{% comment %}
  Dr.Strips+ — "Beat the House" welcome popup
  ============================================
  A tic-tac-toe game against "the house" that ends by revealing an email
  capture, which reveals a discount code — see WIN ODDS below for how
  the outcome is decided.

  Restyled to match the site's Geist / near-black clinical design
  system (same fonts, colors and button conventions as the homepage
  and Science page) instead of the original navy/lime reference.

  TIMING: appears 7 seconds after page load, once per browser session
  (sessionStorage-gated) — see SHOW_DELAY_MS / SHOW_ONCE_PER_SESSION.

  The house now opens with the center square (a short "thinking" beat,
  then an O lands in the middle) as soon as the popup appears, so it's
  immediately obvious this is a live tic-tac-toe game against "the
  house" rather than a static graphic — see botOpensGame().

  WIN ODDS (added Sept 2026): each game rolls generous mode 75% of the
  time and competitive mode the other 25% (see houseCompetitive /
  botOpensGame). In generous mode the bot is provably unable to ever
  complete three in a row — pickBotMove only considers moves that leave
  it a guaranteed path to keep avoiding a win no matter how the visitor
  plays (see botHasNonWinningPath), so generous games always end in a
  player win or a draw, never a house win. In competitive mode the bot
  plays for real — takes a winning move when it has one, blocks the
  player otherwise — so the house has a genuine chance to win (in
  practice, against a reasonably attentive player, a large share of
  competitive-mode games do end in a house win or draw). Either way —
  win, lose, or draw — finishing the game reveals the email capture,
  which reveals the discount code; the outcome only changes the
  messaging shown, never whether the code unlocks.

  DISCOUNT CODE: displays "EARLY". Confirm a matching discount code
  exists in Shopify admin → Discounts before relying on this in
  production — nothing here creates the discount itself.

  EMAIL CAPTURE (added Sept 2026, revised to the reload-based version):
  submitting the email now genuinely subscribes the visitor to
  Shopify's own marketing list, using Shopify's native newsletter
  mechanism — the same `form` tag (type: 'customer') Dawn's own
  footer/email-signup-banner sections use (see the hidden
  #dsp-newsletter-form below) — which creates or updates a Shopify
  customer flagged as accepting email marketing, tagged "newsletter".

  This causes a real, brief page reload: Shopify processes the
  subscription server-side and redirects back to the same page with
  `?customer_posted=true` in the URL. On that reload, this script
  detects the flag, cleans the URL back up, and reopens the popup
  straight on the "code revealed" screen — skipping the game and the
  7-second delay — so the visitor's flow reads as "click reveal →
  quick refresh → here's your code" rather than losing their place.

  Two things were confirmed necessary by testing directly against
  this store, not just by reading Shopify's docs:
  1. A plain JS fetch() POST to /contact is rejected outright with
     "Missing CAPTCHA token" — this store has Shopify's hCaptcha bot
     protection active on the customer/newsletter form, so a real
     a real `form` tag (type: 'customer') has to be used instead of a hand-built
     request.
  2. Even a genuine form submission needs `Shopify.captcha.protect()`
     called on it first, and to be submitted via `requestSubmit()`
     rather than `submit()` (only `requestSubmit()` fires the 'submit'
     event that `protect()` listens on to inject its token) — see
     shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/trust-security/captcha.
     An earlier version of this fix tried submitting into a hidden
     iframe to avoid the page reload entirely, keeping the whole thing
     instant — but that path turned out unreliable in testing (the
     invisible challenge sometimes never resolved, silently dropping
     the signup with no way to detect it). A real top-level submission
     (this version) was consistently reliable, so a brief reload was
     chosen deliberately over an instant experience that could quietly
     lose signups.

  Once Klaviyo's Shopify app is connected, Klaviyo's own sync picks up
  any customer who accepts marketing automatically, so no further
  wiring is needed here when Klaviyo gets connected.
{% endcomment %}
<style>
